        "What Is a Low-Carb Diet for Diabetes Blood Sugar Control and How Does It Affect the Body?",
      body: `<p>A low-carb diet for diabetes blood sugar control is a dietary strategy that limits carbohydrate intake to 20–50 g per day, focusing on protein, healthy fats, and non-starchy vegetables. The goal is to reduce the amount of glucose that enters the bloodstream after meals, thereby lowering post-prandial spikes and improving overall glycemic control.</p>
<h3>Mechanism</h3>
<p>When carbohydrate consumption is reduced, the body's reliance on glucose for energy decreases. This shift promotes the use of fatty acids and ketone bodies as alternative fuels, which can enhance insulin sensitivity and reduce hepatic glucose production.</p>
<p>Lower insulin demand also decreases the risk of insulin resistance, a key driver of type 2 diabetes.</p>
<h3>Secondary Effects</h3>
<p>Beyond blood sugar, a low-carb approach can improve lipid profiles by lowering triglycerides and raising HDL cholesterol. It also tends to increase satiety hormones such as peptide YY, helping to control appetite and support weight loss.</p>
<p>These metabolic benefits contribute to a lower risk of cardiovascular disease.</p>
<h3>Research Note</h3>
<p>Randomized controlled trials have shown that a low-carb diet can lead to meaningful reductions in glycated hemoglobin (HbA1c) and fasting glucose compared with standard care. The evidence suggests that even modest carbohydrate restriction can produce clinically relevant improvements in metabolic health.</p>`,
